ge of 52  tire with 90% of their salary and then  of course if you take into consideration  the cost-of-living increases after that  you can see that over a period of time  they end up getting more in retirement  than they were actually getting while  they were working so it would be silly  for them to keep working after the age  of 50 or 55 and again and compare that  to Social Security we we think of that  as an entitlement something that we can  will get no matter what how is this  different there's a number of elements  and this is this is part of the problem  with trying to explain it to people is  there are so many pieces to the puzzle  one of course is the retirement age and  with Social Security you have everybody  has to wait until 62 with the earliest  and normally 65 and then now 67 which  they moved the the goalposts back for  the coming generations but with fire and  police for example if they have put in  their 30 years by age 50 they can retire  and begin collecting immediately so not  only are they getting more money than  you would get from Social Security by  far they're also starting to collect it  earlier they're also there thousands of  public employees in California that are  making upwards of six figures per year  just in pension benefits CalPERS as you  know will be they're mounting a response  they're catching a lot of heat they say  things like well the average pension is  only about 25 grand per year plus these  folks don't draw on Social Security like  most people do what's your response to  that actually there's several possible  responses the first thing that always  amazes me is that they bring up Social  Security at all because we'd gladly at  this point trade them if they want  Social Security we'll give it to them  and if it's an eliminate the pension but  in terms of the average pension I mean  that is obviously what they're doing is  to get the average they count everybody  they include everybody going back to the  oldest on the list going back 20 30  years the real problem has been created  during the past ten years since 1999  since SB 400 passed since all the  formulas were increased so is it fair to  say when we're comparing the u.s. to  Greece that  everyone's trying to say to what degree  is is this comparison fair it's really a  difference in degree but not a  difference in kind is that fair to say  well I think what I think what people  are looking at is the fact that the debt  is causing all of these problems and the  debt is not just any one thing in this  country for example you have to look at  the social security liability Medicare  than all of the state public pension  plans that are underfunded and then all  of the local pension plans that are  underfunded and to begin seeing this  number you know grow and grow to the  point where at some point we're not  going to be able to pay for those  pensions and do all the other things  that people want to do I want to read  you a quotation from Willie Brown that  I'm sure you've heard many times before  Willie Brown of course used to be  Speaker of the California Assembly for  many years  former San Francisco mayor earlier this  year he said this quote talking about  this is politically unpopular and  potentially even career suicide for most  office holders but at some point someone  is going to have to get honest about the  fact that 80 percent of the state county  and city deficits are due to employee  costs either we do something about it at  the ballot box or a judge will do  something about it in bankruptcy court  what does it say that someone with  liberal credentials like Willie Brown is  talking about this I think he  experienced firsthand what what was  happening and he has seen and continued  to grow in magnitude in fact he was  interviewed about two weeks ago I think  it was a Sacramento TV station and it  was a very brief comment the interviewer  asked him do you think that this will  cause the state to go bankrupt he said 